WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.950
Vsauce! Kevin here, with a game that gives
you quantifiable, verifiable strength against

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:13.220
your opponents and even the advantage of going
first. Simple math and logic conspire to delude

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:20.090
you into believing that the odds are in YOUR
favor -- but in the long run I'm gonna win

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:28.029
every time. And even when you understand how
this paradox works, you STILL probably won't

00:00:28.029 --> 00:00:30.420
be able to rationalize it.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:36.070
It goes like this: I’ve got four different
dice, and I’ve replaced their regular sides

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:43.230
of 1-6 with different combinations of numbers.
Here are some maps to make it easier to see.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:52.830
A has 4’s and 0’s, B has all 3’s, C
has 2’s and 6’s, and D has 5’s and 1’s.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:59.750
You choose one and I’ll choose one. We roll
and the highest number wins. And because I’m

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:05.140
so nice -- I’m gonna let YOU choose the
best die first.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.390
WHICH IS THE BEST?

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:11.750
The simplest, safest way to choose the best
one is to add up the sides and take the highest

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:19.290
total. C has a total of 20. Those 2’s aren’t
great, but the 6’s beat anything. C seems

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:25.970
like the best overall choice. B and D each
add to 18 and A only has 16.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:31.880
But if you choose C, I’ll choose B -- and
I’m gonna win most of the time. LOOK. Here’s

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:38.060
a table of all the possible rolls both of
us can have. Of the 36 possible combinations

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:45.490
of rolls, B comes out on top 24 times to C’s
12. You’ve chosen what looks like the best

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:49.440
die, and I’ll still beat you 2 out of 3
times.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:54.870
Okay. So. Just adding up the sides isn’t
good enough, so maybe you factor in standard

00:01:54.870 --> 00:02:02.060
deviation on the sides of the dice… hold
on... hold on... I’m gonna give you a game-changing

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.920
hint. Here it is:

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:11.830
A beats B. B beats C. and C beats D. These
charts account for every single roll in that

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:17.630
progression, and I’m showing you the data
to prove that A is at the top of a chain of

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:18.830
dominance.

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:24.950
You know that due to the transitive property,
if A beats B and B beats C, then A should

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:32.480
also beat C. And if C beats D, then A and
B should beat D, too. It’s like lining people

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.830
up by height, if A is taller than B and B
is taller than C, then A has to be taller

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:40.580
than C. Makes sense. Right?

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:45.571
Alright. Let’s play again -- and it sounds
like you want to choose A this time? Because

00:02:45.571 --> 00:02:51.490
it beats everything? Because I literally just
showed you that the transitive property makes

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:59.930
A better than B, C and D, RIGHT? WRONG! If
you choose A, I’m gonna take the WORST choice

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:05.849
at the very END of the ‘greater than’
chain. I’m choosing D to go against your

00:03:05.849 --> 00:03:13.330
A, and I’m going to crush you with my D.
How?

