00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:59.840
Okay so here’s why I’m choosing the worst
die to beat your best -- wait. These dice

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:05.810
are too easy. So many numbers repeat that
it’s elementary -- and you’re a genius.

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:11.650
Let’s use another set of numbers from 0
to 12. This set has the same property as the

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:17.609
easier ones, A is better than B, B is better
than C, and C is better than D. If we get

00:04:17.609 --> 00:04:23.400
a tie, we’ll just roll again. You choose
A, the strongest die.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:28.620
I want the weakest die because… it’s actually
not.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:34.900
D is at the losing end of our chain, and my
D die is still going to win nearly ⅔ of

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:59.889
the time against your strong A. Which seems
impossible but let’s test it!

00:04:59.889 --> 00:05:05.270
So despite D seeming to be the weakest and
A being the strongest, out of 15 tolls, D

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:13.410
won exactly twice as many times as A. D is
somehow stronger than A. In this game, no

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:19.550
matter WHAT die you choose, I’m gonna beat
you twice as often as you beat me -- even

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:28.400
with the A &gt; B &gt; C &gt; D chain of dominance,
I am ALWAYS going to pick a better die.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:34.990
Because these dice aren’t transitive. These
are Efron’s Dice. Here’s the proof: My

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:38.159
voice cracked. Who cares? Math!

00:05:38.159 --> 00:05:44.099
These charts reveal the truth about the matchups
in our 0-12 dice, and I’ve labeled the squares

00:05:44.099 --> 00:05:50.069
that each die wins. Just like I said, A beats
B around ⅔ of the time, and it’s the same

00:05:50.069 --> 00:05:53.409
for B vs. C and C vs. D.

00:05:53.409 --> 00:05:59.521
But let’s look at D vs. A -- despite being
worse against C, which is worse than B, which

00:05:59.521 --> 00:06:08.000
is worse than A, D is actually STRONGER than
A. Which makes NO SENSE... IT MAKES NO SENSE.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.919
But it’s mathematically true.

00:06:10.919 --> 00:06:16.849
Bradley Efron is a statistician who has won
just about every award in the statistics game

00:06:16.849 --> 00:06:23.210
over the last 60 years. And he’s still going
-- in 2018 he won the International Prize

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:28.509
in Statistics, which is basically the Nobel
Prize of stats. And he invented what we now

00:06:28.509 --> 00:06:35.600
call Efron’s Dice -- sets of dice that are
maddeningly nontransitive, and that nontransitivity

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:42.610
is cloaked so carefully that it’s almost
impossible to grasp even when you know it’s

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:43.750
true.

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:50.099
If you choose D? Fine, I’ll just choose
C, which we know beats D most of the time.

00:06:50.099 --> 00:06:54.960
If you choose B? I can choose A and win a
lot more than I lose.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:07:02.960
C? I choose B. A? I choose D. You literally
have NO WAY to win this game in the long term,

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:08.849
because I can ALWAYS choose a die that’s
better than yours -- even if you choose the

00:07:08.849 --> 00:07:10.849
best die first.

00:07:10.849 --> 00:07:17.929
And that’s the veridical paradox here -- how
can you have the best possible choice that…

00:07:17.929 --> 00:07:24.069
isn’t? How can a die prove it’s the strongest
and then LOSE to the weakest? How can a die

00:07:24.069 --> 00:07:29.970
verifiably lose to everything except when
it’s up against the one it SHOULDN’T be

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:32.369
able to win against?

00:07:32.369 --> 00:07:38.520
The problem isn’t the dice. It’s us.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:44.210
We fall into a cognitive trap of thinking
in absolute terms when real value is often

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:52.830
relative. In this game, the concept of better
or worse is deceptive -- it depends completely

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:59.129
on the situation. Our brains, especially when
it comes to numbers, want to sort patterns

00:07:59.129 --> 00:08:07.449
and construct algorithms and employ heuristics
that identify the best choice. And we LOVE

00:08:07.449 --> 00:08:14.080
ranking things, whether it’s music or sports
or cereal tier lists.

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:21.930
But… life isn’t always transitive. And
if you get stuck thinking in absolutes, you’re

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:28.919
gonna get duped by the gamemaster who knows
when the real value lies in the relative -- and

00:08:28.919 --> 00:08:32.970
they’re gonna win most of the time.

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:34.370
SO UNSTICK YOUR BRAIN.

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:39.590
And as always -- thanks for watching. I was
gonna throw these at the camera. But that

00:08:39.590 --> 00:08:41.740
